{
  "concepts": [
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition": "Review of each included sprint item under the authority cited by its manifest. Its six outcomes are accepted, local_paydown, design_reframe, requirement_reprice, product_reprice and remove. For the predicate 'the reviewed item is accepted as compliant under the sprint authority', their mappings are respectively accept/satisfied, local_repair/falsified, re_enter/invalid_basis, re_enter/invalid_basis, re_enter/invalid_basis and no matching disposition/falsified. Local debt supplies the local_paydown counterexample while the governing relation remains coherent; a necessary authority change instead requires repricing. Removal supplies a counterexample without requiring a violated governing relation or competing truth path. Repricing names the re-entry layer as a declared domain fact accompanying the core value. Debt-entry outcomes accepted, ticketed, repriced and removed map respectively to accept/satisfied, local_repair/falsified, re_enter/invalid_basis and no disposition/falsified, for the separate predicate 'the debt entry is accepted under the sprint authority'.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line":312,
          "quote":"| `remove` | none | falsified | the work is backed out or kept out of closure; the reason for removal is the counterexample. No disposition of ACTIVATION §3 has this condition, because removal does not require a violated governing relation or a competing truth path |",
          "section_id":"repo://stdo-v3-generation-rc9/claude/c2cfa9b908d1722ae3f26c32fa4ece691083bdc3/source/standards/TICKETS.md#TICKETS%2F3.%20Sprint%20rules%2FClose%20review",
          "start_line":312
        },
        {
          "end_line":325,
          "quote":"sprint authority\": accepted matches `accept` (`satisfied`), ticketed\nmatches `local_repair` (`falsified`), removed takes `falsified` with no\nmatching disposition, and repriced matches `re_enter`\n(`invalid_basis`), on the grounds of `accepted`, `local_paydown`,",
          "section_id":"repo://stdo-v3-generation-rc9/claude/c2cfa9b908d1722ae3f26c32fa4ece691083bdc3/source/standards/TICKETS.md#TICKETS%2F3.%20Sprint%20rules%2FClose%20review",
          "start_line":322
        }
      ],
      "key":"sprint_close_review",
      "label":"Sprint close review and result mappings"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition":"A durable unit of work with one authoritative record. One markdown file is the default carrier; repository or external-tracker carriers are conditional alternatives.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding":[
        {
          "end_line":339,
          "quote":"Each ticket has one durable authoritative record; the default carrier is\none markdown file.",
          "section_id":"repo://stdo-v3-generation-rc9/claude/c2cfa9b908d1722ae3f26c32fa4ece691083bdc3/source/standards/TICKETS.md#TICKETS%2F4.%20Ticket%20rules%2FOne%20durable%20record%20per%20ticket",
          "start_line":338
        }
      ],
      "key":"ticket",
      "label":"Ticket"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition":"One admitted upstream work item with more than one build-tenant execution line, within urn:stdo:bounded-context:build-tenancy. The upstream authority and each tenant-local ticket have distinct identities and lifecycles. The three lawful cases are upstream-only authority with no tenant duplicate yet; a single tenant-local execution line without required siblings; and one upstream authority ticket plus one suffixed tenant-local ticket per execution line.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding":[
        {
          "end_line":359,
          "quote":"A multi-build-tenant work item is one admitted upstream work item with more\nthan one build-tenant execution line. The upstream authority and each\ntenant-local ticket retain distinct identities and lifecycles.",
          "section_id":"repo://stdo-v3-generation-rc9/claude/c2cfa9b908d1722ae3f26c32fa4ece691083bdc3/source/standards/TICKETS.md#TICKETS%2F4.%20Ticket%20rules%2FMulti-build-tenant%20independence",
          "start_line":357
        }
      ],
      "key":"multi_build_tenant_work",
      "label":"Multi-build-tenant work"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition":"The kind of work record. The minimum common set is feature, bug, spike and chore; the default set is expected to suffice for most cases.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding":[
        {
          "end_line":422,
          "quote":"The minimum common set of ticket types is `feature`, `bug`, `spike` and\n`chore`. A project may add more; the default set should be enough for\nmost cases",
          "section_id":"repo://stdo-v3-generation-rc9/claude/c2cfa9b908d1722ae3f26c32fa4ece691083bdc3/source/standards/TICKETS.md#TICKETS%2F4.%20Ticket%20rules%2FTypes",
          "start_line":420
        }
      ],
      "key":"ticket_type",
      "label":"Ticket type"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition":"The execution or migration discipline governing a ticket, orthogonal to type. Its minimum common set is ordinary and implementation_migration; feature, bug, spike and chore can each carry either category.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding":[
        {
          "end_line":431,
          "quote":"Ticket category is orthogonal to ticket type: type answers what kind of\nwork record this is; category answers what execution or migration\ndiscipline governs the ticket (P3, P1; rc.2 #ticket-category). The minimum\ncommon set is `ordinary` and `implementation_migration`, so a `feature`,\n`bug`, `spike` or `chore` may carry either.",
          "section_id":"repo://stdo-v3-generation-rc9/claude/c2cfa9b908d1722ae3f26c32fa4ece691083bdc3/source/standards/TICKETS.md#TICKETS%2F4.%20Ticket%20rules%2FCategory",
          "start_line":427
        }
      ],
      "key":"ticket_category",
      "label":"Ticket category"
    },
    {
      "conditions":["ticket_category is implementation_migration"],
      "definition":"A governed replacement of one implementation truth path by another across producers, consumers, projections and proof surfaces. Examples include replacing a tracker or read-model family with one authoritative carrier, introducing a public interface requiring migration of every producer and consumer before closure, and removing a bridge or compatibility facade from an authoritative contract family.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding":[
        {
          "end_line":438,
          "quote":"`implementation_migration` is\nused when the ticket is not \"change code until tests pass\" but a governed\nreplacement of one implementation truth path with another across producers,\nconsumers, projections and proof surfaces: a bug ticket that replaces an old\ntracker or read-model family with one new authoritative carrier; a feature\nticket that introduces a new public interface and requires every producer\nand consumer to migrate before closure; a refactor ticket that removes a\nbridge path or compatibility facade from an authoritative contract family.",
          "section_id":"repo://stdo-v3-generation-rc9/claude/c2cfa9b908d1722ae3f26c32fa4ece691083bdc3/source/standards/TICKETS.md#TICKETS%2F4.%20Ticket%20rules%2FCategory",
          "start_line":431
        }
      ],
      "key":"migration_ticket",
      "label":"Implementation migration ticket"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition":"The intake-triage declaration of where a change lawfully re-enters the constitutional chain, using SPEC_METHOD §4 classes. Common, nonexclusive pairings are feature with goal_reprice, intent_reprice, product_reprice, requirement_reprice or design_reframe; bug with requirement_reprice, design_reframe or realization_refactor; and chore with design_reframe or realization_refactor. A spike is exploratory but still carries its current triage class.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding":[
        {
          "end_line":464,
          "quote":"Ticket type and change class are orthogonal: type answers what kind of\nwork record this is; change class answers where the change lawfully\nre-enters the constitutional chain. A ticket records the `change_class`\ndeclared by intake triage, using the classes of\n[SPEC_METHOD §4](SPEC_METHOD.md#change-classes) (P4b, P2; rc.2\n#change-class).",
          "section_id":"repo://stdo-v3-generation-rc9/claude/c2cfa9b908d1722ae3f26c32fa4ece691083bdc3/source/standards/TICKETS.md#TICKETS%2F4.%20Ticket%20rules%2FChange%20class",
          "start_line":459
        }
      ],
      "key":"change_class",
      "label":"Change class"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition":"Ticket statuses have the minimum domain backlog, active and completed. Lanes locate those states: backlog is accepted durable work outside current execution focus; active is the current bounded execution set; completed is closed work retained as history. Ticket and local statuses, lanes, sprint statuses, migration_strategy, library_usage and recorded change_class are declared carrier states, not evaluation results.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding":[
        {
          "end_line":540,
          "quote":"The ticket statuses, any local state a project adds, the lanes below, the\nsprint statuses of §3 and the `migration_strategy` and `library_usage`\nvalues of §6 are declared states of their carriers, not results, and map\nto no core value; the recorded `change_class` is a declared state under\n[SPEC_METHOD §4](SPEC_METHOD.md#change-classes).",
          "section_id":"repo://stdo-v3-generation-rc9/claude/c2cfa9b908d1722ae3f26c32fa4ece691083bdc3/source/standards/TICKETS.md#TICKETS%2F4.%20Ticket%20rules%2FStatus",
          "start_line":536
        },
        {
          "end_line":559,
          "quote":"`backlog`\nis accepted durable work that should remain visible but is not part of the\ncurrent execution focus; `active` is the current bounded execution set;\n`completed` is closed work retained as history.",
          "section_id":"repo://stdo-v3-generation-rc9/claude/c2cfa9b908d1722ae3f26c32fa4ece691083bdc3/source/standards/TICKETS.md#TICKETS%2F4.%20Ticket%20rules%2FLanes",
          "start_line":556
        }
      ],
      "key":"ticket_state_and_lane",
      "label":"Ticket states and lanes"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition":"One run-scoped contract for each execution governed by the source method, derived from the smallest lawful carrier for the required lifetime after establishing upstream work authority. Its minimum state domain includes drafted, admitted, rejected and superseded. A ticket-shaped contract describes categorisation, closure criteria, proof surface, non-closure conditions and required contract fields; that shape creates neither durable ticket identity nor ticket-lane state.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding":[
        {
          "end_line":634,
          "quote":"Every execution governed by this method uses one run-scoped execution\ncontract. The required lifetime of the work or obligation selects the\nsmallest lawful source carrier: first establish the applicable upstream\nwork authority, then select the first matching row below. For work inside\nan admitted sprint the local carrier boundary is that sprint; otherwise it\nis the current run",
          "section_id":"repo://stdo-v3-generation-rc9/claude/c2cfa9b908d1722ae3f26c32fa4ece691083bdc3/source/standards/TICKETS.md#TICKETS%2F5.%20From%20carrier%20to%20execution%2FCarrier%20selection",
          "start_line":629
        },
        {
          "end_line":691,
          "quote":"the contract carries at minimum the states named in ACTIVATION §1\n(drafted, admitted, rejected, superseded).",
          "section_id":"repo://stdo-v3-generation-rc9/claude/c2cfa9b908d1722ae3f26c32fa4ece691083bdc3/source/standards/TICKETS.md#TICKETS%2F5.%20From%20carrier%20to%20execution%2FExecution%20contract%20admission",
          "start_line":690
        }
      ],
      "key":"execution_contract",
      "label":"Run-scoped execution contract"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition":"F13 is the source's work-carrier family: selection of the record carrying work, its permitted claims and closure. Instance premises concern lifetimes, several apparent state surfaces, several build tenants, truth-path migrations and variation among files, trackers and services. The table distinguishes consequences, local domain or policy choices, their owners and consumer loci. Cited standing is P0 recovered; P1, P2, P3, P4a and P6 independent; P4b derived from P0 and P2 with the non-reacquirable step-fact premise; and P5 derived from P0 and P6 with the open-world premise. Local choices include carrier kinds and selection, sprint availability and mechanics, fields/types/categories/statuses/lanes/shapes, tenant suffixes, migration declarations and review conventions, repricing states and reconciliation order. Product definition owns carrier bindings, admission mechanism, evidence surface and tenant identity; the governing method owns whether sprint debt can survive.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding":[
        {
          "end_line":586,
          "quote":"This family (F13 in the index of\n[FOUNDATIONS §3 Foundation to law](FOUNDATIONS.md#foundation-to-law))\ndecides which record carries a piece of work, what that record may claim\nand when the work closes. Its instance premises, which the commitments do\nnot supply, are named in the rows as follows: lifetimes, work and its\nobligations last one run, one bounded batch or longer; several surfaces,\ngoals, tickets, comments, boards and summaries can each appear to state\nthe same work state; several tenants, one work item may be realised in\nmore than one build tenant; migrations, some work replaces one\nauthoritative truth path with another across producers, consumers and\nproofs; carriers vary, a project may keep its carriers in files, trackers\nor services. Each commitment's standing is written as the table of\ncommitments in FOUNDATIONS §3 states it.",
          "section_id":"repo://stdo-v3-generation-rc9/claude/c2cfa9b908d1722ae3f26c32fa4ece691083bdc3/source/standards/TICKETS.md#TICKETS%2F5.%20From%20carrier%20to%20execution%2FFoundation%20to%20law%3A%20work%20carriers",
          "start_line":574
        }
      ],
      "key":"work_carrier_family",
      "label":"F13 work-carrier family"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition":"Ticket and work-instruction terms whose meanings resolve through PRODUCT_DEFINITION §5 bounded-context semantic resolution, using the cited product, re-entry surface, execution basis and affected scope.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding":[
        {
          "end_line":736,
          "quote":"Ticket and work-instruction terms resolve under the bounded-context\nsemantic resolution of PRODUCT_DEFINITION §5. A ticket may use an\nunqualified term when its cited product, re-entry surface, execution basis\nand affected scope select exactly one bounded-context meaning (P1, P3;\nrc.2 #bounded-context-term-binding).",
          "section_id":"repo://stdo-v3-generation-rc9/claude/c2cfa9b908d1722ae3f26c32fa4ece691083bdc3/source/standards/TICKETS.md#TICKETS%2F5.%20From%20carrier%20to%20execution%2FBounded-context%20term%20binding",
          "start_line":732
        }
      ],
      "key":"bounded_context_terms",
      "label":"Bounded-context term binding"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition":"Migration declaration state with values inside_out_hard_break and fundamental_re_adoption. The former orders breaks from deepest source truth outward; the latter records explicit human product-authority selection and adjudication of inherited implementation as carry_across, redundant or rewrite.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding":[
        {
          "end_line":800,
          "quote":"The required fields are `migration_strategy`, with the values\n`inside_out_hard_break` and `fundamental_re_adoption`, and\n`library_usage`, with the values `none`, `consume` and `extend`.",
          "section_id":"repo://stdo-v3-generation-rc9/claude/c2cfa9b908d1722ae3f26c32fa4ece691083bdc3/source/standards/TICKETS.md#TICKETS%2F6.%20Implementation%20migration%2FDeclaration%20and%20checklist",
          "start_line":798
        }
      ],
      "key":"migration_strategy",
      "label":"Migration strategy"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition":"Migration declaration state none, consume or extend, recording whether the active boundary rebuilds locally, consumes an existing reusable library/commonisation surface, or extends it before the local implementation lands.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding":[
        {
          "end_line":864,
          "quote":"An implementation migration ticket declares whether the active boundary is\nrebuilding a pattern locally, consuming an existing reusable library or\ncommonisation surface, or extending that surface before the local\nimplementation lands; `library_usage` records which (P2, P4a; rc.2\n#recurring-realization-and-library-declaration).",
          "section_id":"repo://stdo-v3-generation-rc9/claude/c2cfa9b908d1722ae3f26c32fa4ece691083bdc3/source/standards/TICKETS.md#TICKETS%2F6.%20Implementation%20migration%2FRecurring%20realisation%20and%20library%20declaration",
          "start_line":860
        }
      ],
      "key":"library_usage",
      "label":"Library usage declaration"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition":"Assessment distinct from construction output and from the decision owner's acceptance disposition. The reviewer is not the reviewed subject's author. Tier-1 protocol, acceptance and reuse are referenced to EVALUATION §§3,5 and ACTIVATION §7; this member supplies work-carrier requirements and its independent-review boundary policy.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding":[
        {
          "end_line":1044,
          "quote":"The generic law is tier 1: a constructor's self-review is construction\noutput and the independent-assessment protocol is\n[EVALUATION §3](EVALUATION.md#3-independent-assessment); acceptance as the\ndecision owner's disposition, and closure by stated condition rather than\nby reviewer approval, is [EVALUATION §5](EVALUATION.md#5-acceptance); reuse\nof a closed result is [ACTIVATION §7](ACTIVATION.md#7-closed-result-and-reuse).\nThis section states what those laws require of work carriers.",
          "section_id":"repo://stdo-v3-generation-rc9/claude/c2cfa9b908d1722ae3f26c32fa4ece691083bdc3/source/standards/TICKETS.md#TICKETS%2F7.%20Authority%2C%20judgment%2C%20closure%20and%20state",
          "start_line":1038
        }
      ],
      "key":"independent_assessment",
      "label":"Independent assessment"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition":"Distinct reusable records of residual judgment J and owner ruling O, supported by computed facts C as applicable. J records a question, exact subject/basis, facts/evidence, actor/authority, conclusion/scope, uncertainty and concrete revising observation. O records original wording, owner, exact subject/basis, permitted scope/effects, conditions and source separately from agent interpretation. Repricing dispositions retained, superseded, deferred and withdrawn are owner-set obligation states, not core results, candidate acceptance or predecessor-claim qualification.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding":[
        {
          "end_line":1115,
          "quote":"A residual judgment that another activation needs is recorded once, with\nits owning work, in the existing carrier selected under §5. The record\nidentifies the question; the exact subject and governing basis; the\nsupporting computed facts and evidence; the responsible actor and its\nauthority; the conclusion and its scope; the uncertainty; and a concrete\nrevising observation that lets a consumer determine whether the conclusion\nstill applies.",
          "section_id":"repo://stdo-v3-generation-rc9/claude/c2cfa9b908d1722ae3f26c32fa4ece691083bdc3/source/standards/TICKETS.md#TICKETS%2F7.%20Authority%2C%20judgment%2C%20closure%20and%20state%2FRecorded%20judgment%20and%20owner-ruling%20continuity",
          "start_line":1109
        }
      ],
      "key":"judgment_and_owner_ruling",
      "label":"Recorded judgment and owner ruling"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition":"A bounded work claim whose completion is evaluated over every applicable declared obligation, exact subject/basis/scope, applicable judgments and rulings, and active non-closure conditions. The source identifies this condition with FOUNDATIONS §4 conjunction of results and makes this section its owner; SPEC_METHOD's consistency gate applies it over a change span.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding":[
        {
          "end_line":1186,
          "quote":"A work claim is complete only when every applicable declared obligation is\nsatisfied by valid evidence for its exact subject, basis and scope; every\nrequired judgment and owner ruling is present and applicable; and no active\nnon-closure condition remains. A mandatory obligation whose applicability is\nunknown, or whose evidence is insufficient, withholds the dependent claim;\nevidence presence alone is not satisfaction. The existing owner decides any\nreserved acceptance",
          "section_id":"repo://stdo-v3-generation-rc9/claude/c2cfa9b908d1722ae3f26c32fa4ece691083bdc3/source/standards/TICKETS.md#TICKETS%2F7.%20Authority%2C%20judgment%2C%20closure%20and%20state%2FCondition-based%20closure%20and%20reuse",
          "start_line":1180
        }
      ],
      "key":"work_claim",
      "label":"Work claim and condition-based closure"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition":"A proof projection from admitted work authority. The recommended substantive-test derivation is ticket → intake triage → lawful re-entry point → governing requirement, design, module, published function, carrier or closure surface → test expectation. Implementation behavior is evidence, not the authority determining the expected result.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding":[
        {
          "end_line":1222,
          "quote":"ticket -> intake triage -> lawful re-entry point -> governing requirement,\ndesign, module, published function, carrier, or closure surface -> test expectation",
          "section_id":"repo://stdo-v3-generation-rc9/claude/c2cfa9b908d1722ae3f26c32fa4ece691083bdc3/source/standards/TICKETS.md#TICKETS%2F7.%20Authority%2C%20judgment%2C%20closure%20and%20state%2FTest%20case%20authority",
          "start_line":1221
        }
      ],
      "key":"test_case",
      "label":"Test case authority"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition":"One work-state authority chain: goals own the bounded work wave, tickets own ticket state, milestone authority owns subordinate milestone state, and accepted design owns realisation decisions. Comments, dashboards, registers, indexes and generated summaries are projections. Surface questions remain distinct: goals identify the current broad wave; SPEC_METHOD identifies constitutional class/re-entry; sprints identify a bounded proof/evidence/paydown batch; tickets identify durable work and state; comments record proposals, arguments, decisions, reviews and handoffs; POSTING_GUIDE specifies comment publication structure and naming.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding":[
        {
          "end_line":1267,
          "quote":"Work state has one authority chain: goals own the bounded work wave, the\nticket owns ticket state, milestone authority owns subordinate milestone\nstate (§1), and accepted design owns realisation decisions. Comments,\ndashboards, registers, indexes and generated summaries are projections\n(P6, P3; rc.2 #one-truth-state-projection-and-drift-refusal-stdo-up-009).",
          "section_id":"repo://stdo-v3-generation-rc9/claude/c2cfa9b908d1722ae3f26c32fa4ece691083bdc3/source/standards/TICKETS.md#TICKETS%2F7.%20Authority%2C%20judgment%2C%20closure%20and%20state%2FOne-truth%20state%20projection%20and%20drift%20refusal%20%28UP-009%29",
          "start_line":1263
        },
        {
          "end_line":1445,
          "quote":"The goals surface: what broad work wave\nmatters now. SPEC_METHOD: what constitutional change class and re-entry\npoint govern the work. Sprints: what bounded execution batch is currently\npricing proof, evidence and paydown. Tickets: what durable units of work\nexist and what state they are in. Comments: what was proposed, argued,\ndecided, reviewed or handed off. POSTING_GUIDE: how comment-layer\npublications are structured and named.",
          "section_id":"repo://stdo-v3-generation-rc9/claude/c2cfa9b908d1722ae3f26c32fa4ece691083bdc3/source/standards/TICKETS.md#TICKETS%2F9.%20Operating%20mode%20and%20relationships%2FRelationship%20to%20other%20surfaces",
          "start_line":1439
        }
      ],
      "key":"work_state_authority_chain",
      "label":"Work-state authority and projections"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition":"Three recommended document shapes, presented as examples rather than instantiated work. The sprint example SPRINT-2026-04-30-operational-projection is active under goal operational-projection-ux, with opened/updated timestamps 2026-04-30T09:00:00Z, references to GOALS, PRODUCT and a governing design surface, and scope limited to layout/readability/interaction/visual evidence. It excludes product truth, data contract, runtime/governance semantics and new constructive carriers; expects realization_refactor; includes UXI-001 relation-line readability work, screenshot evidence and pending contrast debt; and illustrates timebox/surface-volume/design-lead close triggers, forensic walkthrough, screenshots/notes, deferred screenshots/accessibility/replay review, hidden-drift non-closure and paydown/repricing. The ordinary example T-001, Add Product Layer To SPEC_METHOD, is an active feature/ordinary ticket for methodology-bootstrap with product_reprice, product re-entry, high priority and triage/creation/update date 2026-04-07. Its acceptance concerns PRODUCT.md in the chain, intent → product → requirements bootstrap, and aligned examples/reconstruction, with a comment link. The migration example B-010 is an active bug/implementation_migration ticket using inside_out_hard_break and library_usage none because the boundary-specific pattern is not recurring; goal operator-gap-truth, design_reframe, design_surface re-entry and date 2026-04-20. It replaces fallback gap projection with synthetic convergence fields by declared-obligation projection plus separately classified fallback gaps, within the operator-query acceptance path and with no excluded scopes. It identifies old/new producers, shared query/summary/review consumers, gap/span/negative-proof derived surfaces, the removal-of-old-authority closure law, and an unchecked migration checklist.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding":[
        {
          "end_line":1279,
          "quote":"These shapes are the recommended carriers of the fields and sections the\nrules above require; a project may bind another shape that carries the\nsame fields (§2; P3, P4b; rc.2 #recommended-ticket-shape).",
          "section_id":"repo://stdo-v3-generation-rc9/claude/c2cfa9b908d1722ae3f26c32fa4ece691083bdc3/source/standards/TICKETS.md#TICKETS%2F8.%20Recommended%20shapes",
          "start_line":1277
        },
        {
          "end_line":1400,
          "quote":"- closure_law: migration closes only when the old projection is no longer authoritative within the affected scope and mixed old/new proof does not count as acceptance",
          "section_id":"repo://stdo-v3-generation-rc9/claude/c2cfa9b908d1722ae3f26c32fa4ece691083bdc3/source/standards/TICKETS.md#TICKETS%2F8.%20Recommended%20shapes",
          "start_line":1400
        }
      ],
      "key":"recommended_carrier_shapes",
      "label":"Recommended sprint and ticket templates"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
