      "key":"mixed_state_negative_evidence",
      "statement":"Positive behavior proof is insufficient; the proof surface should include at least one negative/structural proof that the old path cannot satisfy closure law. Examples are rejecting the severed seam before accepting downstream repair as progress; failing closed if the new carrier is removed; rejecting legacy payloads without admitted carriers; resolving deliberately conflicting old/new fixtures in favor of new truth or failing closed; rejecting structurally different same-name carriers/functions/routes/policies/projections; and avoiding undeclared local fixtures or mutable sibling workspaces in required closure proof. Keep the ticket open if the old path normally works in scope or remains reachable on the claimed acceptance path, subject to explicit exceptions. Where an interface checklist exists, negative proof must exercise named public consumers relevant to closure; bypassing one legacy shim is insufficient if helpers/loaders/rebuild paths already bypass it.",
